WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.583
(upbeat music)

00:00:05.337 --> 00:00:06.188
(air whooshing)

00:00:06.188 --> 00:00:08.771
(upbeat music)

00:00:10.257 --> 00:00:12.070
(air whooshing)

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:13.436
- I'm Tina Hesman-Saey.

00:00:13.436 --> 00:00:16.920
I'm a Senior Writer at
Science News Magazine,

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.693
and I cover Molecular Biology.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.210
- And I'm Erin Garcia de Jesús,

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:24.500
a General Assignment
Reporter at Science News,

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.810
but since January of 2020,

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:28.890
Tina and I have mostly been covering

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.550
the coronavirus and Covid-19.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:34.697
- We are here because
Science News is turning 100,

00:00:35.631 --> 00:00:39.080
and as part of our centennial celebration,

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:40.810
we're talking to scientists about

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:43.510
some big ideas in their fields.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:47.600
We have with us now four experts,

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.910
four scientists whose research

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:54.010
is helping us learn to
combat the coronavirus,

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:56.223
and I'll let them introduce themselves.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:58.250
- Hi, I'm Mark Denison.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.220
I'm a professor of Pediatrics

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:01.940
at Vanderbilt University Medical Center

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.100
in Nashville, Tennessee,

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.760
and I have been studying
coronavirus since,

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.440
I guess since 1984,

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.780
mostly focusing on their
replication targets

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.150
and more recently on antivirals.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.840
- Hi, my name is Akiko Iwasaki.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.220
I'm a professor of Immunobiology

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.020
at the Yale University School of medicine.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.270
I'm also an investigator

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.720
of the Howard Hughes Medical Institute.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.860
My lab studies antiviral immunity.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:33.120
Prior to studying SARS-CoV-2,

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.090
we were studying different
viruses including,

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.490
herpes simplex virus, influenza,

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.950
Zika, rhinovirus, and others.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.260
And since March of 2020,

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:47.723
we pivoted to studying SARS-CoV-2
infection and immunity.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:53.290
- Hi, my name is David Martinez,

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:56.700
I'm a Postdoctoral
Scholar in the Baric lab

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.002
at the University of North Carolina.

00:01:59.002 --> 00:02:01.830
In addition to that, I
am a Hannah Gray Fellow:

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.050
The Howard Hughes Medical Institute.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.061
And prior to Covid-19,

00:02:06.061 --> 00:02:09.070
then I had also studied viral immunity

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:12.680
to a number of different
viruses including HIV,

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.350
and Dengue virus as a Flavivirus model.

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:19.520
And at the start of the Covid-19 epidemic,

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:24.000
then our lab rapidly shifted our focus

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.840
to this novel coronavirus,

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.730
and we've been studying

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:32.120
the immunity to SARS-CoV-2
and even SARS related viruses

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:37.023
and just the greater beta
coronavirus genus ever since.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.027
- Hi, I'm Kanta Subbarao.

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:43.890
I'm the Director of a
WHO collaborating Center

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.210
for Reference and Research on Influenza

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:47.043
in Melbourne, Australia.

00:02:47.043 --> 00:02:48.970
And I'm the professor

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:51.040
in the Department of
Microbiology and Immunology

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.100
at the University of Melbourne.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:57.290
I was previously at
the NIH for many years.

00:02:57.290 --> 00:02:59.850
I've worked on influenza
for most my career,

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:02.420
and started working on SARS actually

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:07.120
with the great introduction
by Mark Denison back in 2002,

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:09.350
when I was at the NIH

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:12.630
and we had a very active SARS
program for about five years

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:16.480
working with the Baric Lab and with Mark.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.560
And about five years
ago, I moved to Australia

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.650
where I primarily work on influenza.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:26.270
But of course since the
emergence of SARS-CoV-2,

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.233
we've been looking on SARS 2 as well.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:32.150
- Fantastic.

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:33.230
Thanks all of you

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:35.460
for those great
introductions of yourselves.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.100
We're so excited to learn

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.150
and hear what you guys have to say today.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.130
Akiko, our first question
is actually for you.

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.810
So you mentioned that before the pandemic,

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:46.650
you were not working on coronaviruses,

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:48.570
you pivoted in March, 2020.

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:51.310
And then I was wondering if
you could tell us a little bit

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:54.053
about what that was like
to pivot your research.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:59.770
- Yeah, so this was quite
abrupt, as you can imagine.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:02.470
Until like, as I said, March of 2020,

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:06.070
we were working on a
variety of different viruses

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:07.910
and also cancer immunology

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:11.490
and everyone was doing their own thing.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:15.190
And of course the school was shut down

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:17.510
for research other than

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:20.330
those that are dedicated
to studying SARS-CoV-2.

00:04:20.330 --> 00:04:23.050
And of course as an viral immunologist,

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:27.370
I was very motivated to
study this new corona virus

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:29.930
and trying to understand
the immune responses

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:32.570
that are happening in the patients.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:37.570
And so we set up a biorepository
called Impact Yale,

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:40.950
where we would collect
longitudinal biospecimen

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:43.120
from patients in the hospital.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.260
And that's how we sort of started to study

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:49.400
immune responses as they
happen in the patients.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.470
And every day was an excitement

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:56.370
because we learned, all kinds
of things on a daily basis.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:58.500
And at the same time it was very scary

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:01.150
because we didn't have
a vaccine at the time.

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:04.200
I wanted to make sure
my lab members are safe.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.824
We took every precaution we can,

00:05:06.824 --> 00:05:10.120
and thankfully none of us got infected,

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:12.513
but it was also a scary time for everyone.

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:15.310
- Well, Mark,

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:19.700
you obviously were studying coronaviruses

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:22.070
and you mentioned that
you've been working on

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:24.230
some antivirals.

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:26.720
Maybe you can talk a little bit about

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:31.720
how we have developed treatments
against the coronavirus

00:05:32.424 --> 00:05:34.713
and where you see that going next.

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:36.870
- Oh, let's see, this is,

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:38.540
what do we have, like 12 hours,

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:39.632
is that what we have here?

00:05:39.632 --> 00:05:41.850
(Tina laughs)

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:45.690
Well, our focus has really
been on the viral polymerase

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:47.520
and on the novel

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:52.130
proofreading extra nuclease
the coronaviruses in code,

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:54.900
which was really first
described in a virus infection

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:57.467
in my lab, back in 2007.

00:05:57.467 --> 00:05:59.870
And that had sort of led me to

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:02.200
the interacting with other scientists,

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:03.970
and particularly polymerase biologists,

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:05.870
like Olve Peersen at Colorado State,

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:09.300
and Craig Cameron, who was
at Penn State at the time

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:10.330
and saying up,

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:13.520
because this enzyme seemed
to be knocking out viral,

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:17.490
the capacity of nucleoside
analogs to inhibit coronaviruses,

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:19.900
we wanted to try to find
compounds that would work

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:23.080
just really biochemically
or in genetically,

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.740
to really inhibit the virus replication.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:28.210
So we had been really working on

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:29.990
fundamental principles of replication

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:32.020
and starting in around 2013,

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:34.850
we established our
collaboration with Gilead

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:37.843
and dealt with our long-term
collaboration with UNC.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:40.510
And when we discovered that

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:42.170
this compound which we didn't know about

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:43.134
what it was at first was,

00:06:43.134 --> 00:06:46.770
'cause we were working
blindly was Remdesivir,

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:50.130
and showed that this inhibited
the virus dramatically.

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:54.090
We then really pivoted
back starting in 2014,

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:56.120
to thinking about the capacity of these

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:58.570
to actually inhibit coronaviruses broadly.

00:06:58.570 --> 00:06:59.460
We were interested at the time,

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:02.900
of course in MERS and using
our model viruses as well,

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:03.733
and SARS.

00:07:03.733 --> 00:07:06.450
And so SARS, Covid, and then MERS,

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:07.960
in working at these and showing this.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.770
And I think our enthusiasm just
really sort of grew and grew

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:13.250
as this first Remdesivir

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:15.480
and then the drug EIDD 1931

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:16.680
that became Molnupiravir

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:20.960
both showed this really
impressive dramatic capacity

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:23.150
to bypass this proofreading function

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:24.850
inhibit through virus replication.

00:07:26.014 --> 00:07:30.620
I started learning what I
called learning drug development

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:32.280
as the second language late in life

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:35.529
where I wasn't very smart,
but I was really enthusiastic.

00:07:35.529 --> 00:07:39.100
And we were also seeing the
capacity of these viruses,

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:42.050
of other bad viruses, et
cetera, to infect human cells.

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:45.410
So we started sort of pounding the drum

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:47.980
and standing up on the
street corner and preaching

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:49.770
that pandemics were coming,

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:51.837
and that we needed to
be prepared for them.

00:07:51.837 --> 00:07:53.620
And so we were really,

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:55.980
just positioned us to be able to do that

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:57.810
and publishing our work
right before this started,

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:59.700
I think was just,

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:01.610
like it's unusual to call it serendipity

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:02.670
in the face of a pandemic,

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:05.090
but we're grateful that we
were in a position to do that.

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:06.723
So I think where we're going is,

00:08:08.533 --> 00:08:10.930
right now obviously the
NIH and the whole world

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:12.690
is really interested in
this issue of particular

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:14.560
orally available antivirals

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.810
targeting multiple different targets

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:17.643
that can be broadly

00:08:17.643 --> 00:08:19.430
active against coronaviruses.

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:22.350
At least the virus, the
family of coronaviruses,

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:24.610
if not more broadly against
other viruses as well.

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:26.510
So I think this renewed emphasis

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:29.360
and sort of this dramatic
focus around antivirals

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:31.600
is important because I
think we're seeing that,

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:33.000
as good as the vaccines are going to be,

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:34.700
we're still going to have
people who are at risk,

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:36.060
people who are immunocompromised,

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:40.100
people who need either prophylaxis
or they need treatment.

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:42.330
And that's something
that we're very excited

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:44.500
to continue to try to probe.

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:47.710
- Right. And so, I mean, there's been,

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:50.970
obviously Remdesivir is now approved

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:55.563
for treating Covid-19 for
people who are in the hospital,

00:08:56.685 --> 00:08:57.535
and molnupiravir,

00:08:59.248 --> 00:09:00.081
(Mark and Tina laughing)

00:09:00.081 --> 00:09:01.331
- Molnupiravir.

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:05.480
- Molnupiravir, which I learned recently

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:07.603
is named for Thor's hammer.

00:09:07.603 --> 00:09:09.250
- Well that's maybe apocryphal,

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:11.150
but that's what I was
told by one of the people

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:12.420
who developed it.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:14.580
Mjölnir is Thor's hammer.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:16.810
So that's where I heard it came.

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:18.480
- Right. So anyway,

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:22.600
that has recently had
some encouraging results

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:26.120
in clinical trials

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:28.196
for people who have been newly diagnosed.

00:09:28.196 --> 00:09:29.793
- Mm-hmm.

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:34.460
- Why didn't we have
these antivirals before?

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:37.580
I mean, we've had
coronaviruses around forever.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:39.100
- Well, I don't know that

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:41.580
there was more than one or two
funded programs in the world

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:45.203
working on coronavirus
antivirals before the pandemic.

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:48.560
And maybe we may have been the
only one in the United States

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:52.360
that I'm aware of that was
focused specifically on that.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:53.460
I think part of it is because

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:55.240
antivirals are fundamentally difficult.

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:56.790
They're more difficult than vaccines.

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:58.340
So apologies to vaccinologists.

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:01.903
And the reason for that
is because viruses,

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:05.610
you can work out, a vaccine
to work outside of the cell.

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:07.380
An antiviral has to work inside of a cell

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:10.830
since viruses are obligate
intracellular parasites,

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:12.260
and they use host cell mechanism.

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:14.720
So anything you do with an antiviral

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:17.270
fundamentally is going to be
targeting the inside of a cell

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:19.740
and mechanisms that may be
common to the cell and the virus.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:24.740
So, and also viruses are
exceptionally good at evading these

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:26.760
and finding something
that can damage the virus

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:31.030
without damaging the cell
is profoundly challenging.

00:10:31.030 --> 00:10:33.690
I think maybe a final answer
is because there had never been

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:35.630
sort of a Manhattan project type approach

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:36.550
where everybody in the world

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:38.280
was looking at all their compounds,

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:39.820
was really willing to target this,

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:41.590
and that the resources
were brought to bear,

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:43.700
to bring immunologists, animal model,

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:45.940
geneticists, virologists,

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:48.850
biochemists, structural biologists,

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:50.640
evolutionary biologists,

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:53.300
together to try to understand quickly,

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:54.690
do we have targets that could work

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:56.990
and how do we develop those rapidly?

00:10:56.990 --> 00:10:58.600
- Right.

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:00.640
Well, I want to switch over

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:03.140
and talk to Kanta a little bit about,

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:06.200
you know, this is a pandemic it's global,

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:09.970
and you're in Australia on
the other side of the world,

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:12.643
from the rest of us who
are in the United States.

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:16.550
Can you tell us a little bit about

00:11:16.550 --> 00:11:19.930
what the experience has
been like in Australia

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:21.420
and for the rest of the world,

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:23.550
because you've been one of the people

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:27.410
who have been monitoring
the spread of the virus

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:29.570
and, you know, what are you seeing,

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:32.410
especially in terms of variants

00:11:32.410 --> 00:11:36.950
and in what we can expect
out of this coronavirus.

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:38.040
- Sure.

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:40.620
I mean, I think that the
experience of the pandemic

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:43.420
has been very different in
different parts of the world.

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:47.890
Australia is a huge island,

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:50.400
and they closed their borders very early.

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:54.570
We closed our borders in February
of last year, completely.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:56.860
So there was no further,

00:11:56.860 --> 00:11:58.800
there was very little introduction

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:01.260
of the virus into Australia

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:03.350
and the same was true in

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:07.490
New Zealand, in Taiwan,
and Hong Kong, Singapore,

00:12:07.490 --> 00:12:09.830
a lot of countries closed their borders.

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:11.650
So their experience of the pandemic

00:12:11.650 --> 00:12:13.120
is really quite different

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:17.840
from what the US, the
UK, Europe and so on saw.

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:19.740
What we also did in Australia

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:23.640
was they instituted a
mandatory two week quarantine

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:25.840
for anybody returning to the country,

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:27.400
or coming into the country.

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:29.870
So that two week period
was actually long enough

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:33.930
to essentially capture any
infection that was brought in.

00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:38.403
So seeding of the community
just wasn't happening very much.

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:41.320
That did happen when we had breakdowns

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:45.280
in the hotel quarantine
system, which did happen,

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:46.600
but we've had,

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:48.080
you know, we were really protected

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:52.240
through the first year and
something of this pandemic.

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:53.810
However, the Delta variant

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:55.400
has really changed all of that

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:58.330
because it is far more infectious.

00:12:58.330 --> 00:13:01.650
And I don't know if it's
partly pandemic fatigue,

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:04.820
Australians have been
incredibly compliant.

00:13:04.820 --> 00:13:08.620
I must say having spent most
of my adult life in America,

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:11.150
it's, you know, wearing a mask

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:13.350
was just not a political statement.

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:15.833
It was just done because
we were asked to do it,

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:20.430
and people were very compliant
with all of the restrictions.

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:22.653
So the country did very well.

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:26.100
Unfortunately now of
course we're dealing with

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:29.480
the fact that, you know, Delta
is here and it's spreading.

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:31.450
We're dealing with the fact that Australia

00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:33.710
got a little complacent

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:35.680
and instituted

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:40.743
and actually deployed
vaccine rather slowly.

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:45.710
So we were pretty late
to the party for vaccines

00:13:45.710 --> 00:13:47.860
and only now are really catching up.

00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:50.480
So now we've overtaken the United States

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:53.960
in the number that are fully vaccinated.

00:13:53.960 --> 00:13:57.020
And again, maybe it's the fact that

00:13:57.020 --> 00:13:58.930
Aussies are a little more compliant,

00:13:58.930 --> 00:14:03.210
but in many parts of the
country, more than 80%,

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:06.360
well over 80% of the population
has had their first dose,

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:09.210
and will get their second dose
within the next few weeks.

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:11.160
So I think we will reach,

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:13.590
and Canberra which is
the country's capital

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:17.110
has over 99% of first vaccination.

00:14:17.110 --> 00:14:19.670
So they will be the most vaccinated city

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:21.840
in the world probably soon.

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:25.440
So, you know, it's been quite different

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:28.840
and it's been a little
heartbreaking for me to watch

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:31.690
the pandemic transpire in the US.

00:14:31.690 --> 00:14:34.800
I worked for the us government
for most of my adult life,

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:38.130
and it's been really
quite devastating to watch

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:41.780
the size of the pandemic
and the fact that,

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:43.407
there was such a breakdown

00:14:43.407 --> 00:14:46.900
in the support for public
health over the years,

00:14:46.900 --> 00:14:50.390
that essentially this pandemic
was such a full-blown event

00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:51.743
in the United States.

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:54.760
And even now when the numbers
are coming under control,

00:14:54.760 --> 00:14:57.150
it's still far greater than

00:14:58.550 --> 00:15:01.670
Australia has ever seen
through the whole pandemic.

00:15:01.670 --> 00:15:04.470
So in a sense, you know, sitting far away,

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:08.130
it's been a bit of a mixed bag of

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:09.150
how I feel about it.

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:10.277
I mean, a lot of people here say,

00:15:10.277 --> 00:15:12.320
"Oh, aren't you just so glad you're here?"

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:16.530
And I think, no. Everybody I
know is in America (laughs).

00:15:16.530 --> 00:15:18.570
And I, you know, it's
very hard to be far away.

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:20.530
My family is there.

00:15:20.530 --> 00:15:21.850
So it's hard to be far away

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:23.930
and observe this from a distance.

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:26.210
But on the other hand, you
know, in terms of science,

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:27.363
it's been amazing.

00:15:28.612 --> 00:15:29.563
And, you know,

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:34.360
I spent huge number of my nighttime hours

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:36.800
on zoom calls and phone calls,

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:40.870
and the progress of science has been,

00:15:40.870 --> 00:15:42.270
you know, just breathtaking.

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:46.060
- Yeah. Kanta, you
mentioned the Delta variant.

00:15:46.060 --> 00:15:49.200
I'm curious what your thoughts are about,

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:50.420
you know, things were looking better

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:52.110
before the Delta variant came.

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:53.600
There were other variants as well

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:55.100
that people were concerned about.

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:58.120
Like, what are your thoughts
on how things would have gone

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:02.281
had that like, particularly
the Delta variant not

00:16:02.281 --> 00:16:03.114
(indistinct)

00:16:03.114 --> 00:16:04.230
- So that's a good question.

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:05.770
I mean, it's a hypothetical

00:16:05.770 --> 00:16:07.780
because of course the Delta
variant is very much here

00:16:07.780 --> 00:16:11.354
and has taken over all over the world.

00:16:11.354 --> 00:16:13.520
And I don't think it's going
to be the last variant we see.

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:16.187
I think we will see
additional variants emerge.

00:16:16.187 --> 00:16:18.810
And I think the reason
we're seeing variants

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:21.350
is because of the burden of infection

00:16:21.350 --> 00:16:23.270
in the population around the world.

00:16:23.270 --> 00:16:26.250
And we could talk about vaccine equity,

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:28.500
and third doses and so on,

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:31.730
but until we vaccinate more of the world,

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:35.820
we're going to see infections
in unvaccinated people

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:37.762
and the emergence of variants.

00:16:37.762 --> 00:16:40.230
So, without the Delta variant,

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:42.423
the vaccines were looking fantastic.

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:46.580
And they continue to do very well

00:16:46.580 --> 00:16:48.830
in protection against severe disease.

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:50.100
So if the initial goal,

00:16:50.100 --> 00:16:51.540
and the initial goal of vaccination

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:54.140
was to prevent severe illness and death,

00:16:54.140 --> 00:16:56.430
and to protect our healthcare systems.

00:16:56.430 --> 00:16:57.870
And they've done very well with that,

00:16:57.870 --> 00:16:59.590
even with the Delta variant.

00:16:59.590 --> 00:17:02.160
Now, I think if the Beta
variant had actually taken off

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:03.140
and spread around the world,

00:17:03.140 --> 00:17:05.140
we would have been an even worse trouble

00:17:05.140 --> 00:17:06.540
because the Beta variant actually

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:09.710
is less well inhibited

00:17:09.710 --> 00:17:14.220
by the antibody elicited by
the original Wuhan strain.

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:16.280
Against the Delta variant
I think the big issue

00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:18.380
is that it is highly infectious,

00:17:18.380 --> 00:17:20.620
and that even people
that are fully vaccinated

00:17:20.620 --> 00:17:22.120
can get infected.

00:17:22.120 --> 00:17:25.570
Now, I think it is not entirely clear yet

00:17:25.570 --> 00:17:28.020
how much a fully vaccinated person

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:30.450
who gets infected with the Delta variant,

00:17:30.450 --> 00:17:32.840
what is the risk of them
actually spreading it

00:17:32.840 --> 00:17:34.870
to other fully vaccinated people?

00:17:34.870 --> 00:17:38.670
I think that is a piece
of data we don't have yet.

00:17:38.670 --> 00:17:41.310
We do know that vaccine induced immunity,

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:44.670
the antibody titers
are dropping with time.

00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:49.480
We know that the Delta variant
is very fit and is spreading.

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:51.430
We also know the fully vaccinated people

00:17:51.430 --> 00:17:54.710
can get infected with the Delta variant,

00:17:54.710 --> 00:17:57.780
and they can shed virus at least by PCR

00:17:57.780 --> 00:18:01.130
at a similar note as
an unvaccinated person.

00:18:01.130 --> 00:18:04.670
But the bit of data that I don't see yet

00:18:04.670 --> 00:18:06.603
is how many people,

00:18:06.603 --> 00:18:09.323
how many fully vaccinated people,

00:18:09.323 --> 00:18:12.090
a fully vaccinated person can infect.

00:18:12.090 --> 00:18:14.330
So I think our hope is to actually

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:17.820
get the level of
vaccination up in the world

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:20.953
so that we can drive down some
of this burden of infection.

00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:24.240
- Speaking of vaccines and variants,

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:26.021
this brings me to David.

00:18:26.021 --> 00:18:29.920
You and your colleagues are working on,

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:31.490
like, next generation vaccines,

00:18:31.490 --> 00:18:33.420
thinking about the future of the world

00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:35.120
where we have lots of different variants,

00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:37.730
or even other emerging coronaviruses

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:40.140
and working on something that
could tackle all of those

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:42.283
in a universal coronavirus vaccine.

00:18:44.081 --> 00:18:47.060
What do you think have we learned

00:18:47.060 --> 00:18:50.040
about the potential for
these types of shots

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:50.873
from your work?

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:57.500
- Yeah, so I think so far based
on all the data we've seen,

00:18:58.060 --> 00:19:03.060
then achieving at least
a more broad vaccine,

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:08.120
at least against the
cousins of SARS-CoV-2,

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:12.120
might be a more readily
achievable thing to do.

00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:16.910
So we've been able to at
least pre-clinically develop

00:19:16.910 --> 00:19:21.010
vaccine concepts against
SARS related viruses,

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:24.360
including SARS-CoV-2 and
the critical variants

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:27.580
such as the Beta variant
that Kanta mentioned,

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:30.810
that is relatively more
neutralization resistant

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:32.800
compared to other variants.

00:19:32.800 --> 00:19:36.040
So in terms of the broad spectrum vaccines

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:40.090
against the SARS related
viruses or the Sarbecoviruses,

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:44.960
then I think that might be
a easier target to achieve.

00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:48.210
So we have a couple of
different vaccine concepts

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:49.830
that we've been involved with.

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:51.513
And so far,

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:56.720
both of those concepts appear to be

00:19:56.720 --> 00:19:58.240
highly protective against

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:01.830
a number of different of
those viral infections.

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:06.370
I think moving forward, what
will be a more difficult target

00:20:06.370 --> 00:20:09.850
to hit will be a more broad,

00:20:09.850 --> 00:20:13.050
or a Beta coronavirus
vaccine, for example,

00:20:13.050 --> 00:20:16.110
that not only covers this
SARS related viruses,

00:20:16.110 --> 00:20:19.550
but also includes for example,
group 2c coronaviruses,

00:20:19.550 --> 00:20:22.783
which include MERS, or even
the common cold coronaviruses.

00:20:25.060 --> 00:20:28.800
- So you mean we might not
have to have colds anymore?

00:20:30.038 --> 00:20:31.940
- That would be ideal.

00:20:31.940 --> 00:20:34.250
And that of course remains
an aspirational goal.

00:20:34.250 --> 00:20:35.660
But I think moving forward,

00:20:35.660 --> 00:20:38.750
that is certainly where
we would all like to get

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:40.230
in terms of

00:20:40.230 --> 00:20:44.720
developing a Beta coronavirus
widely protective vaccine

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:46.230
that could not only

00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:49.750
prevent the infection from SARS-CoV-2,

00:20:49.750 --> 00:20:52.950
but also more distant coronaviruses.

00:20:55.100 --> 00:20:56.110
But of course,

00:20:56.110 --> 00:21:00.550
that is currently being
tested preclinically.

00:21:00.550 --> 00:21:04.197
So we don't yet know if
that'll be possible in humans.

00:21:05.310 --> 00:21:06.640
- Can you talk a little bit about

00:21:06.640 --> 00:21:10.010
what makes these universal
vaccines different

00:21:10.010 --> 00:21:13.550
from the ones that we have
now against the coronavirus?

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:17.150
I mean, most of them are directed
against the spike protein,

00:21:17.150 --> 00:21:21.943
or they are killed whole viruses.

00:21:23.091 --> 00:21:25.810
Is that kind of approach you're taking,

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:29.080
like you get a spike protein

00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:31.450
that is one size fits all,

00:21:31.450 --> 00:21:35.110
or are you doing a
different type of approach?

00:21:35.110 --> 00:21:38.130
- So a number of different
approaches are being tried.

00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:40.310
We've been involved in
at least two of them.

00:21:40.310 --> 00:21:42.400
And one of the approaches that

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:44.353
we've been primarily involved in,

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:48.190
is actually expanding
the breadth of the spike

00:21:48.190 --> 00:21:51.420
by admixing the different
immunogenic domains,

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:54.010
so the NTD, the RBD, and the S2

00:21:54.010 --> 00:21:55.860
in generating chimeric spikes,

00:21:55.860 --> 00:21:59.530
and then with the advent
of the mRNA technology,

00:21:59.530 --> 00:22:01.130
then you're able to multiplex

00:22:01.130 --> 00:22:03.080
the delivery of these vaccines.

00:22:03.080 --> 00:22:04.170
So that is one approach that

00:22:04.170 --> 00:22:09.170
has shown immense early success,
at least in mine so far.

00:22:09.890 --> 00:22:13.750
And another approach that
we've also been involved in

00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:17.270
is the delivery of a nanoparticle vaccine

00:22:17.270 --> 00:22:21.630
that expresses the RBD
of SARS related viruses,

00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:26.300
and the RBD of SARS-CoV-2 for example,

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:30.730
actually shares a highly
conserved signature

00:22:30.730 --> 00:22:33.460
with a number of different
SARS related viruses.

00:22:33.460 --> 00:22:37.280
So we've actually identified
monoclonal antibodies

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:39.180
that are broadly protective against

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:41.770
a number of different
SARS related viruses.

00:22:41.770 --> 00:22:45.250
And if you deliver the part of the RBD

00:22:45.250 --> 00:22:48.360
that has this broadly protective epitope,

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:51.030
then you can readily induce
broadly protective immunity

00:22:51.030 --> 00:22:52.000
in mice.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:56.580
So what's important and what's
promising is that you have,

00:22:56.580 --> 00:23:00.100
at least you have more than one platforms

00:23:00.100 --> 00:23:01.570
that can achieve this.

00:23:01.570 --> 00:23:04.283
And I think that raises optimism for me.

00:23:05.510 --> 00:23:08.460
- Yeah. Well, Akiko,

00:23:08.460 --> 00:23:12.000
you know, we've been talking
about vaccine immunity,

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:14.150
but you've been studying a lot about

00:23:14.150 --> 00:23:16.660
what goes wrong in the body,

00:23:16.660 --> 00:23:19.310
or what just happens in the body

00:23:19.310 --> 00:23:21.770
when you get infected
with the coronavirus.

00:23:21.770 --> 00:23:23.690
Can you talk a little bit about

00:23:23.690 --> 00:23:25.839
what you've been learning

00:23:25.839 --> 00:23:30.839
and how is this virus response different

00:23:30.920 --> 00:23:35.513
than other coronaviruses, or
just other respiratory viruses?

00:23:36.890 --> 00:23:40.050
- Yeah. So one of the first learnings

00:23:40.050 --> 00:23:43.400
that we got from studying these patients

00:23:43.400 --> 00:23:47.250
was that there this, in the severe cases,

00:23:47.250 --> 00:23:51.990
the patients are generally
unable to control the virus well,

00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:54.030
especially quick enough.

00:23:54.030 --> 00:23:57.520
And what happens is that once
the patients failed to do so,

00:23:57.520 --> 00:23:59.940
the virus spreads into the lung

00:23:59.940 --> 00:24:03.390
and damages some of the
very delicate tissues

00:24:03.390 --> 00:24:07.050
that are required for oxygen,

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:08.890
you know, gas exchange and so on,

00:24:08.890 --> 00:24:12.830
and that sort of leading to
this pneumonia phenotype.

00:24:12.830 --> 00:24:14.640
And when we study those patients,

00:24:14.640 --> 00:24:16.500
what we're finding is that

00:24:16.500 --> 00:24:19.620
there is misfiring of the immune response,

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:23.150
that normally we think
of antiviral immunity

00:24:23.150 --> 00:24:25.950
mediated by one branch
of the immune response

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:28.260
which is called a type one immunity.

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:29.960
But what we're seeing is that

00:24:29.960 --> 00:24:32.010
those patients are also engaging

00:24:32.010 --> 00:24:34.130
type two and type three immunity,

00:24:34.130 --> 00:24:37.940
which are normally reserved for
fighting parasite infection,

00:24:37.940 --> 00:24:41.110
or a fungal infection,
or bacterial infection.

00:24:41.110 --> 00:24:43.260
And so what appears to us

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:46.243
is sort of like a misfiring
of the immune response.

00:24:46.243 --> 00:24:49.470
They're trying to contain
this virus, you know,

00:24:49.470 --> 00:24:53.120
with any tools that we have on hand,

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:56.760
but actually that's resulting
in the pathological outcome

00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:58.430
in some of these patients,

00:24:58.430 --> 00:25:02.300
because it's not a well-intended
antiviral mechanism.

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:04.850
So, that was the first insight we obtained

00:25:04.850 --> 00:25:07.870
from studying the patients in real time.

00:25:07.870 --> 00:25:12.120
And later on, we also
studied sex differences

00:25:12.120 --> 00:25:15.480
in immune responses that
occur in these patients.

00:25:15.480 --> 00:25:17.720
And, you know, as we all know,

00:25:17.720 --> 00:25:22.170
male sex is a risk factor
for severe and lethal Covid.

00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:25.210
And what we learned is that

00:25:25.210 --> 00:25:27.590
in the early phase of the immune response,

00:25:27.590 --> 00:25:31.970
women tended to generate
more robust T Cell immunity,

00:25:31.970 --> 00:25:35.680
whereas men failed to do so

00:25:35.680 --> 00:25:40.070
but they instead engaged
inflammatory responses.

00:25:40.070 --> 00:25:42.140
And that may have led to

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:45.590
sort of the lack of control of the virus

00:25:45.590 --> 00:25:49.230
that leads to this misfiring
later on that I just mentioned.

00:25:49.230 --> 00:25:51.970
So we also learned that
there's a difference

00:25:51.970 --> 00:25:54.740
in the way male and female

00:25:54.740 --> 00:25:56.680
immune responses are
dealing with the virus.

00:25:56.680 --> 00:25:58.710
And that may explain to some extent

00:25:58.710 --> 00:26:01.040
some of the, you know,
different risk factors

00:26:01.040 --> 00:26:03.701
that we're seeing based on sex.

00:26:03.701 --> 00:26:05.087
- There's also,

00:26:05.087 --> 00:26:08.010
a lot of people have
been talking more about

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:11.370
long-term effects of viral infections,

00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:13.250
and long-term symptoms that you might have

00:26:13.250 --> 00:26:17.430
after you've, you know,
recovered from the,

00:26:17.430 --> 00:26:19.240
the virus infecting your body,

00:26:19.240 --> 00:26:22.696
it's supposedly gone, but you
still have a lot of symptoms,

00:26:22.696 --> 00:26:23.570
you know, long Covid.

00:26:23.570 --> 00:26:24.980
And I know you've been really involved

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:26.640
in that particular field as well.

00:26:26.640 --> 00:26:28.570
So can you talk a little bit about

00:26:28.570 --> 00:26:30.270
what you've learned in that arena?

00:26:31.270 --> 00:26:32.770
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:26:32.770 --> 00:26:36.860
So that's what I'm most
concerned about right now is the,

00:26:36.860 --> 00:26:41.200
you know, the lack of
diagnosis, and care, and therapy

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:44.010
that can help people with long Covid.

00:26:44.010 --> 00:26:46.700
And so long Covid can happen

00:26:46.700 --> 00:26:50.160
in a large fraction of
hospitalized patients

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:51.623
who are discharged alive,

00:26:52.720 --> 00:26:56.810
anywhere estimated between
50 to 70% of these people

00:26:56.810 --> 00:26:58.990
are still having lingering symptoms

00:26:58.990 --> 00:27:01.690
after months of discharge.

00:27:01.690 --> 00:27:03.980
And long Covid can also happen

00:27:03.980 --> 00:27:06.430
in people who had milder infection,

00:27:06.430 --> 00:27:08.130
who were never hospitalized,

00:27:08.130 --> 00:27:12.030
but those patients may
also develop long Covid.

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:14.430
The estimated percentages

00:27:14.430 --> 00:27:17.280
varies but between five to 30%.

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:21.510
So even if we take a
conservative estimate of 5%,

00:27:21.510 --> 00:27:23.050
that's a large number of people,

00:27:23.050 --> 00:27:24.790
given how many millions of people

00:27:24.790 --> 00:27:26.880
are infected with this virus.

00:27:26.880 --> 00:27:28.420
And the latter type

00:27:28.420 --> 00:27:31.000
is also, there are more women

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:34.810
in middle-age that's
enriched in that population.

00:27:34.810 --> 00:27:37.820
And so we currently have
a couple of hypothesis

00:27:37.820 --> 00:27:40.350
we're trying to dissect.

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:43.140
One is that the long Covid can happen

00:27:43.140 --> 00:27:46.970
as a result of a potentially,
a viral reservoir,

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:49.890
or some fragments of virus RNA,

00:27:49.890 --> 00:27:53.410
or protein that deposits in some tissue

00:27:53.410 --> 00:27:56.240
that's causing chronic inflammation.

00:27:56.240 --> 00:28:00.230
The other hypothesis
we're pursuing is that

00:28:00.230 --> 00:28:04.610
this acute infection causes,
triggers auto immune disease,

00:28:04.610 --> 00:28:07.240
which can have long-term consequences,

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:09.800
which involve multiple organ systems.

00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:13.230
And so long Covid has at
least 200 different symptoms

00:28:13.230 --> 00:28:14.560
that have been reported,

00:28:14.560 --> 00:28:17.400
involving like, pretty
much every organ system

00:28:17.400 --> 00:28:18.810
you can imagine.

00:28:18.810 --> 00:28:21.250
And so we really need to find out

00:28:21.250 --> 00:28:22.850
what's going on in these patients

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:24.393
so we can treat them better.

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:29.623
- Are there good treatments at this point?

00:28:29.623 --> 00:28:32.250
- No. I wish there were
some good treatments.

00:28:32.250 --> 00:28:34.470
I'm not sure if the antivirals

00:28:34.470 --> 00:28:36.610
would actually work in these patients.

00:28:36.610 --> 00:28:39.560
If it is actually a
persistent virus infection,

00:28:39.560 --> 00:28:43.500
that might have some, you
know, beneficial effect.

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:45.840
But we have to remember the long Covid

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:49.050
is likely going to be a
heterogeneous disease.

00:28:49.050 --> 00:28:51.860
That it's not one thing that's
causing all these symptoms.

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:53.880
So if we can identify

00:28:53.880 --> 00:28:56.440
let's say, cluster of patients that

00:28:56.440 --> 00:28:59.190
may be driven by persistent virus,

00:28:59.190 --> 00:29:01.670
versus those that are
driven by autoimmunity

00:29:01.670 --> 00:29:03.810
or a combination thereof,

00:29:03.810 --> 00:29:07.120
we can start to think
about rational therapy

00:29:07.120 --> 00:29:08.490
for these people.

00:29:08.490 --> 00:29:11.070
But first is really to
identify what's going on

00:29:11.070 --> 00:29:13.190
so we can have a better therapy.

00:29:13.190 --> 00:29:16.510
And the one thing that
seems to be helping patients

00:29:16.510 --> 00:29:18.940
is the vaccination actually.

00:29:18.940 --> 00:29:22.210
So there are now reports
demonstrating that,

00:29:22.210 --> 00:29:25.180
between 40 to 60% of the long-haulers

00:29:25.180 --> 00:29:27.210
that received the vaccine

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:30.000
feel improvement in their symptoms,

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:32.690
whereas about 20% are
getting worse symptoms.

00:29:32.690 --> 00:29:36.100
So, that gives us a little bit of idea

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:38.170
as to what kind of triggers

00:29:38.170 --> 00:29:39.930
might improve their symptoms

00:29:39.930 --> 00:29:43.400
and what we can do to help them.

00:29:43.400 --> 00:29:44.520
- So I have a question.

00:29:44.520 --> 00:29:46.350
Do you think that that means that

00:29:46.350 --> 00:29:48.200
this implicates

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:51.523
persistent virus infection,

00:29:55.491 --> 00:29:57.180
or is it causing a downregulation

00:29:57.180 --> 00:30:00.930
of a particular immune
response that's detrimental?

00:30:00.930 --> 00:30:02.800
- Yeah. So Mark, that's

00:30:02.800 --> 00:30:06.450
both possibilities are
currently holding up.

00:30:06.450 --> 00:30:07.283
It's, you know,

00:30:08.350 --> 00:30:10.110
one possibility is just simply that

00:30:10.110 --> 00:30:12.940
the vaccine is inducing really good

00:30:12.940 --> 00:30:15.750
immune response that
clears any viral reservoir,

00:30:15.750 --> 00:30:18.870
in which case you can have
like a permanent cure.

00:30:18.870 --> 00:30:22.120
Some people are getting a
permanent cure from vaccine.

00:30:22.120 --> 00:30:24.610
Well, vast majority of
people are not getting that.

00:30:24.610 --> 00:30:27.410
They're getting a temporal
improvement in their symptoms,

00:30:27.410 --> 00:30:30.050
and it's not that all of
their symptoms go away.

00:30:30.050 --> 00:30:32.307
So, that to me tells me that

00:30:32.307 --> 00:30:35.560
potentially it's maybe
cytokines or other factors

00:30:35.560 --> 00:30:37.850
that's induced by the vaccination,

00:30:37.850 --> 00:30:40.840
that's sort of acting
on autoreactive cells

00:30:40.840 --> 00:30:43.390
to set down their cytokine production

00:30:43.390 --> 00:30:45.820
or potentially antibody production,

00:30:45.820 --> 00:30:48.500
but it's not a permanent solution.

00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:50.840
So we really need to
understand like what's going on

00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:53.690
in these patients so we can
provide a permanent solution.

00:30:55.300 --> 00:30:56.430
- Tina, could I comment on that

00:30:56.430 --> 00:30:58.631
in terms of related to antivirals

00:30:58.631 --> 00:31:01.010
really briefly,

00:31:01.010 --> 00:31:01.960
you know, I, and I think

00:31:01.960 --> 00:31:03.930
the nice thing for one
of these kinds of forums

00:31:03.930 --> 00:31:05.450
is to take us a little bit beyond

00:31:05.450 --> 00:31:07.210
and not be afraid to sort of

00:31:07.210 --> 00:31:09.870
think and speculate a
little bit because I think

00:31:09.870 --> 00:31:14.870
we're also deluded with data
and information every day.

00:31:15.440 --> 00:31:17.140
Well, one thing for sure is that

00:31:17.140 --> 00:31:19.430
antivirals work best when
there's a viral target.

00:31:19.430 --> 00:31:21.830
They don't work at all if
there're direct acting antivirals

00:31:21.830 --> 00:31:22.820
that are targeting the virus.

00:31:22.820 --> 00:31:24.520
They don't work at all if
there's no virus present,

00:31:24.520 --> 00:31:27.270
or if the disease is not viral related.

00:31:27.270 --> 00:31:30.230
And I think when, you
know, Remdesivir was used

00:31:31.470 --> 00:31:33.030
late in infection because at the time

00:31:33.030 --> 00:31:36.450
of course at the height of
the epidemic pre vaccines,

00:31:36.450 --> 00:31:38.150
the issue was, can we save people's lives?

00:31:38.150 --> 00:31:40.200
Can we get them off ventilators?

00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:42.330
And so, it's that fact that it was IV

00:31:42.330 --> 00:31:43.270
also encouraged that.

00:31:43.270 --> 00:31:44.900
And so I was actually surprised that

00:31:44.900 --> 00:31:45.990
it had any impact at all

00:31:45.990 --> 00:31:47.817
because all the studies that
had been done in Ralph's lab,

00:31:47.817 --> 00:31:49.863
and my lab and others had shown that

00:31:49.863 --> 00:31:51.800
you use the drug early and it worked,

00:31:51.800 --> 00:31:52.633
you use it late and it doesn't work,

00:31:52.633 --> 00:31:56.490
dnd that was true with both
Molnupiravir and Remdesivir.

00:31:56.490 --> 00:31:57.530
And I've been thinking about this

00:31:57.530 --> 00:31:59.120
in terms of persistent virus infection,

00:31:59.120 --> 00:32:01.330
'cause the very first models that I,

00:32:01.330 --> 00:32:04.890
the lab I went in and Stanley
Perlman's lab in 1984.

00:32:04.890 --> 00:32:07.350
He was working on a model
of viral persistence

00:32:07.350 --> 00:32:09.123
and mouse hepatitis virus whereas,

00:32:10.340 --> 00:32:13.860
if the dams were immunized
and the infants were infected,

00:32:13.860 --> 00:32:16.900
they would not develop
a severe acute disease,

00:32:16.900 --> 00:32:18.340
but they would develop persistent disease

00:32:18.340 --> 00:32:20.930
that led to chronic demyelinating
encephalitis, right?

00:32:20.930 --> 00:32:23.240
So led to demyelination of the brain.

00:32:23.240 --> 00:32:25.410
And that was the model
that I was working on,

00:32:25.410 --> 00:32:28.060
mouse hepatitis virus for all those years,

00:32:28.060 --> 00:32:30.510
which was really an immunological model

00:32:30.510 --> 00:32:32.730
of some ways but it also was associated

00:32:32.730 --> 00:32:35.140
with long-term persistent infection.

00:32:35.140 --> 00:32:37.530
And so I think this is
particularly interesting

00:32:37.530 --> 00:32:40.770
because you theoretically
might have a place for

00:32:40.770 --> 00:32:42.640
an effective antiviral that decreased,

00:32:42.640 --> 00:32:45.760
or that allowed you to
significantly decrease viral load

00:32:45.760 --> 00:32:48.800
over a long period of time
that could be taken early.

00:32:48.800 --> 00:32:50.790
I hadn't thought of that
actually until this moment,

00:32:50.790 --> 00:32:53.460
I'd been thinking prophylactically
or very early in therapy,

00:32:53.460 --> 00:32:55.330
but there might be a
role of that associated

00:32:55.330 --> 00:32:56.710
with in fact with immunization

00:32:56.710 --> 00:32:59.120
because they're not mutually exclusive.

00:32:59.120 --> 00:33:01.340
So, that's an interesting idea.

00:33:01.340 --> 00:33:03.100
- Yeah. I love that thought.

00:33:03.100 --> 00:33:05.940
I mean, what we need is
a good clinical trial.

00:33:05.940 --> 00:33:07.130
- Yes.
- You know.

00:33:07.130 --> 00:33:08.070
- Yes.

00:33:08.070 --> 00:33:09.670
- And I would even say,

00:33:09.670 --> 00:33:11.300
Mark, adding to that, you know,

00:33:11.300 --> 00:33:14.100
potentially another concept
that could be explored

00:33:14.100 --> 00:33:16.590
in conjunction with antivirals

00:33:16.590 --> 00:33:21.080
could be Fc region optimized
monoclonal antibodies,

00:33:21.080 --> 00:33:24.330
optimized for killing of
virally infected cells.

00:33:24.330 --> 00:33:26.769
- Okay. You have to unpack that.

00:33:26.769 --> 00:33:28.597
(David and Tina laughing)

00:33:28.597 --> 00:33:30.310
what does that mean?

00:33:30.310 --> 00:33:31.947
Those words.

00:33:31.947 --> 00:33:32.780
Fc what?

00:33:34.024 --> 00:33:36.060
- So, antibodies,

00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:38.013
so I'm an antibody person.

00:33:39.190 --> 00:33:42.940
So monoclonal antibodies have
two different major regions.

00:33:42.940 --> 00:33:46.290
They have the antigen binding region.

00:33:46.290 --> 00:33:48.700
So the fab region or the Fc region,

00:33:48.700 --> 00:33:50.180
which is a part on the antibody

00:33:50.180 --> 00:33:52.600
that actually talks to the immune system.

00:33:52.600 --> 00:33:56.120
So a number of different recent papers

00:33:56.120 --> 00:33:58.550
from different groups
have demonstrated that,

00:33:58.550 --> 00:34:01.510
the Fc region is actually required for

00:34:01.510 --> 00:34:04.090
not only protection, but you can actually

00:34:05.250 --> 00:34:07.440
improve the function of these antibodies

00:34:07.440 --> 00:34:09.330
if you make them,

00:34:09.330 --> 00:34:11.410
if you modify them in a way that

00:34:11.410 --> 00:34:14.470
they can talk to the immune
system a little bit better.

00:34:14.470 --> 00:34:16.860
So, that could potentially be

00:34:18.060 --> 00:34:20.690
an additive therapy
that you could explore,

00:34:20.690 --> 00:34:24.790
not just in the context of
a long Covid for example,

00:34:24.790 --> 00:34:28.130
but if you were to maybe
combine that with an antiviral,

00:34:28.130 --> 00:34:31.720
so now you have an antiviral
that's targeting the virus

00:34:31.720 --> 00:34:34.370
and you have a different type of biologic

00:34:34.370 --> 00:34:35.800
that's still anti-viral,

00:34:35.800 --> 00:34:38.050
but it's actually engaging
the immune system.

00:34:39.220 --> 00:34:42.950
- So that would be different
from the monoclonal antibodies

00:34:42.950 --> 00:34:44.440
that we've heard about,

00:34:44.440 --> 00:34:48.770
like the ones that are being
used for early treatments.

00:34:48.770 --> 00:34:52.360
- Correct. So, even without modifying,

00:34:52.360 --> 00:34:54.400
even without optimizing

00:34:54.400 --> 00:34:57.370
the part in the antibody that
talks to the immune system,

00:34:57.370 --> 00:35:00.900
the antibodies will do
their thing anyways.

00:35:00.900 --> 00:35:03.900
So this would, like,
you're correctly surmise,

00:35:03.900 --> 00:35:05.450
this would actually include,

00:35:05.450 --> 00:35:08.360
for example, the AstraZeneca cocktail,

00:35:08.360 --> 00:35:10.300
or the Regeneron cocktail,

00:35:10.300 --> 00:35:12.430
or the Bristol-Myers Squibb cocktail.

00:35:12.430 --> 00:35:14.850
So this could potentially be,

00:35:14.850 --> 00:35:17.140
at least, you know, in this
small proportion of patients

00:35:17.140 --> 00:35:19.410
that Akiko mentions that

00:35:19.410 --> 00:35:20.410
could have symptoms,

00:35:20.410 --> 00:35:23.330
long Covid symptoms that
are driven by the virus,

00:35:23.330 --> 00:35:26.600
then this could potentially
be an additional therapy

00:35:26.600 --> 00:35:28.250
that could be explored.

00:35:28.250 --> 00:35:32.370
- Well. I do want to get
all of your thoughts on

00:35:33.690 --> 00:35:37.260
what made this coronavirus

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:39.020
into a pandemic virus?

00:35:39.020 --> 00:35:41.350
I mean, I've been writing a long time

00:35:41.350 --> 00:35:42.860
about infectious diseases,

00:35:42.860 --> 00:35:45.500
and there's been many
different candidates,

00:35:45.500 --> 00:35:48.560
you know, mostly different
influenza viruses,

00:35:48.560 --> 00:35:51.860
you know, eating influenzas particularly.

00:35:51.860 --> 00:35:54.970
And none of them have
really become the pandemic

00:35:54.970 --> 00:35:56.550
that was promised.

00:35:56.550 --> 00:36:00.120
And then this one just sort
of came out of the blue

00:36:00.120 --> 00:36:03.170
and suddenly it was everywhere.

00:36:03.170 --> 00:36:06.680
So, what have we learned about this virus?

00:36:06.680 --> 00:36:11.050
What makes it so special that
it can be a pandemic virus?

00:36:11.050 --> 00:36:12.830
- I think the scary thing is that

00:36:12.830 --> 00:36:15.830
nothing makes it so special
from other coronaviruses.

00:36:19.063 --> 00:36:21.798
- I think, well, I'll wait
till Mark finishes and jump in.

00:36:21.798 --> 00:36:22.631
Go ahead.

00:36:22.631 --> 00:36:25.212
- I'll let you, go ahead
Kanta (indistinct).

00:36:25.212 --> 00:36:28.740
- To me, the two big differences are,

00:36:28.740 --> 00:36:32.100
from all the avian influenza viruses

00:36:32.100 --> 00:36:35.910
that had failed to start a
pandemic, or SARS or MERS,

00:36:35.910 --> 00:36:37.240
there're two things:

00:36:37.240 --> 00:36:40.160
One is that this virus spreads efficiently

00:36:40.160 --> 00:36:43.580
from person to person
by the airborne route,

00:36:43.580 --> 00:36:46.900
which did not happen with MERS or SARS.

00:36:46.900 --> 00:36:49.850
The second really big difference is that

00:36:49.850 --> 00:36:52.290
in both SARS and MERS,

00:36:52.290 --> 00:36:55.760
everybody who was infected had symptoms,

00:36:55.760 --> 00:36:58.090
and with this virus infection,

00:36:58.090 --> 00:37:00.540
we have a lot of asymptomatic individuals,

00:37:00.540 --> 00:37:02.640
people who don't even
know they're infected,

00:37:02.640 --> 00:37:04.150
who are spreading the virus.

00:37:04.150 --> 00:37:06.779
So to me, those were
the two game changers.

00:37:06.779 --> 00:37:11.779
I first heard about the
person to person transmission

00:37:11.890 --> 00:37:14.690
at the very end of January of 2020.

00:37:14.690 --> 00:37:17.197
And I was at a flu meeting and I said,

00:37:17.197 --> 00:37:19.497
"Oh my goodness, this sounds likes flu.

00:37:19.497 --> 00:37:21.870
"This does not sound like SARS at all."

00:37:21.870 --> 00:37:25.280
And to me, that is really
what made the difference

00:37:25.280 --> 00:37:30.000
in making this virus
able to cause a pandemic.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:32.120
- I think Kanta and I were
having these conversations

00:37:32.120 --> 00:37:32.953
in 2003,

00:37:34.930 --> 00:37:37.990
is if SARS escapes, so
we were watching it,

00:37:37.990 --> 00:37:41.610
if it escapes into Indian sub-continent,

00:37:41.610 --> 00:37:42.950
or the African continent,

00:37:42.950 --> 00:37:45.730
or in places where their public health,

00:37:45.730 --> 00:37:47.420
we were concerned, there
might be public health,

00:37:47.420 --> 00:37:51.030
you know, the control issues
in some places in the world,

00:37:51.030 --> 00:37:54.930
war zones, et cetera,
that it would take off.

00:37:54.930 --> 00:37:57.790
And certainly you saw
super spreading events

00:37:57.790 --> 00:37:59.270
and you saw, we all thought

00:37:59.270 --> 00:38:01.360
that that was going to be the one,

00:38:01.360 --> 00:38:03.410
But it had Achilles' heel,

00:38:03.410 --> 00:38:06.339
and that Achilles' heel
was a required symptomatic,

00:38:06.339 --> 00:38:09.540
severe respiratory
infection for transmission.

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:11.260
And I think that that one Achilles' heel,

00:38:11.260 --> 00:38:14.400
the other thing is it's efficient.

00:38:14.400 --> 00:38:16.810
So there was this issue of
efficiency of transmission

00:38:16.810 --> 00:38:17.643
without symptoms.

00:38:17.643 --> 00:38:21.400
And second is that it,
really never rose very high

00:38:21.400 --> 00:38:23.460
except for the super spreading events.

00:38:23.460 --> 00:38:25.490
So those two things really
made it controllable

00:38:25.490 --> 00:38:26.620
by public health measures

00:38:26.620 --> 00:38:29.260
that haven't been
successful with with Covid.

00:38:29.260 --> 00:38:30.660
When I said nothing is special about it,

00:38:30.660 --> 00:38:32.770
well, I was, I shouldn't
be cavalier about it.

00:38:32.770 --> 00:38:33.740
What I meant was

00:38:34.900 --> 00:38:37.860
that we don't understand in coronaviruses

00:38:37.860 --> 00:38:40.370
what would make one
different from another.

00:38:40.370 --> 00:38:41.850
There's no obvious signal,

00:38:41.850 --> 00:38:43.760
except for the potential capacity

00:38:43.760 --> 00:38:45.420
of this particular spike protein

00:38:45.420 --> 00:38:48.100
to show this kind of dramatic variation.

00:38:48.100 --> 00:38:49.690
But when you look at it,

00:38:49.690 --> 00:38:51.550
it looks like another family member.

00:38:51.550 --> 00:38:53.020
You put them side by side,

00:38:53.020 --> 00:38:55.500
and they're going to look
really, really similar.

00:38:55.500 --> 00:38:57.070
The number of nucleotide differences

00:38:57.070 --> 00:38:59.740
across the replicates gene
is exceptionally small

00:38:59.740 --> 00:39:02.290
for they're highly related.

00:39:02.290 --> 00:39:04.570
And so what makes it unique?

00:39:04.570 --> 00:39:06.300
We don't know and that's what I meant

00:39:06.300 --> 00:39:07.770
from the virological perspective,

00:39:07.770 --> 00:39:10.490
but I think from the
perspective of differences

00:39:10.490 --> 00:39:11.323
we've been worrying

00:39:11.323 --> 00:39:14.090
and all of our crying
from the mountain tops

00:39:14.090 --> 00:39:17.680
was about, MERS was, this
is transmitted from camels,

00:39:17.680 --> 00:39:19.710
there's limited transmission
from human to human,

00:39:19.710 --> 00:39:22.970
but if it gains the capacity
for direct respiratory spread,

00:39:22.970 --> 00:39:26.120
it was maintaining a 30
to 40% mortality rate

00:39:26.120 --> 00:39:29.600
over about an eight year
period of people who got it.

00:39:29.600 --> 00:39:32.120
And we were concerned that
this was one that will escape.

00:39:32.120 --> 00:39:34.800
And then MERS seems to
have had the bad habits

00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:39.800
of both SARS Covid and MERS
Covid, but we do not know why.

00:39:39.880 --> 00:39:43.628
And I think that's what
is the important point.

00:39:43.628 --> 00:39:44.750
- And I think virologically,

00:39:44.750 --> 00:39:47.370
that's true for influenza viruses as well.

00:39:47.370 --> 00:39:52.370
We do not yet understand what
makes one influenza virus,

00:39:52.690 --> 00:39:56.140
highly transmissible from person
to person and another not.

00:39:56.140 --> 00:39:59.930
And that's been an area
of interest and study

00:39:59.930 --> 00:40:03.290
because seasonal influenza
viruses transmit very well.

00:40:03.290 --> 00:40:06.810
Avian influenza viruses
generally do not, fortunately,

00:40:06.810 --> 00:40:08.920
and there are avian influenza viruses

00:40:08.920 --> 00:40:10.963
like H5N1 and H7N9

00:40:10.963 --> 00:40:14.305
that have very, very
high case fatality rates.

00:40:14.305 --> 00:40:17.060
But fortunately do not spread efficiently

00:40:17.060 --> 00:40:18.490
from person to person.

00:40:18.490 --> 00:40:20.520
So there's been a lot of
interest in the flu community

00:40:20.520 --> 00:40:21.870
as well to try to understand

00:40:21.870 --> 00:40:24.560
what those determinants
of transmission are,

00:40:24.560 --> 00:40:26.560
so that we can then

00:40:26.560 --> 00:40:31.120
do a better job at risk assessment

00:40:31.120 --> 00:40:35.260
when viruses emerge in, or
cause to zoontic infections,

00:40:35.260 --> 00:40:37.620
to know that this one is one to watch

00:40:37.620 --> 00:40:40.490
and contain immediately, versus another

00:40:40.490 --> 00:40:42.800
that, you know, we could actually

00:40:42.800 --> 00:40:47.203
have small outbreaks and still
not be at risk of a pandemic.

00:40:48.391 --> 00:40:50.970
- So one thing that I've
written a lot about this year

00:40:50.970 --> 00:40:54.860
is the fact that this does seem to be

00:40:54.860 --> 00:40:57.243
transmitted through the air,

00:40:58.400 --> 00:41:02.470
whereas we didn't use to think
that that was sort of the,

00:41:02.470 --> 00:41:07.190
the major route that respiratory
viruses were transmitted,

00:41:07.190 --> 00:41:10.870
which to me I guess now it
doesn't really make sense

00:41:10.870 --> 00:41:14.110
that they wouldn't be
transmitted through the air,

00:41:14.110 --> 00:41:19.110
but is there anything
else that we've learned

00:41:19.200 --> 00:41:21.540
that you think is surprising about that.

00:41:21.540 --> 00:41:24.380
- So this is an area that is fascinating,

00:41:24.380 --> 00:41:27.010
and it's an area where

00:41:27.010 --> 00:41:30.800
it's been very clear that
virologists and physicians

00:41:30.800 --> 00:41:34.700
speak a very different language
from aerosol biologists.

00:41:34.700 --> 00:41:36.290
They both know a lot,

00:41:36.290 --> 00:41:38.870
but they spoke a completely
different language,

00:41:38.870 --> 00:41:42.220
use different terminology,
different measures.

00:41:42.220 --> 00:41:45.530
And so the medical
community basically said

00:41:45.530 --> 00:41:48.880
that anything, that it was
all based on particle size,

00:41:48.880 --> 00:41:51.890
and that if a particle was
more than five microns in size,

00:41:51.890 --> 00:41:53.931
that you emit from the respiratory track

00:41:53.931 --> 00:41:58.040
will settle out to the
air within three meters,

00:41:58.040 --> 00:42:00.680
within three feet or one meter,

00:42:00.680 --> 00:42:03.060
and that's why hospital beds were always

00:42:03.060 --> 00:42:04.720
more than a meter apart,

00:42:04.720 --> 00:42:07.500
and that any virus particle that stayed,

00:42:07.500 --> 00:42:09.040
that was under five microns

00:42:09.040 --> 00:42:11.170
could remain suspended in the air,

00:42:11.170 --> 00:42:13.140
could travel greater distances,

00:42:13.140 --> 00:42:15.710
and was then aerosol transmissible.

00:42:15.710 --> 00:42:18.850
Aerosol scientists tell us
that is complete nonsense,

00:42:18.850 --> 00:42:22.310
that the particle size
is more like 100 microns,

00:42:22.310 --> 00:42:23.580
not five,

00:42:23.580 --> 00:42:28.530
and that the size of the particles changes

00:42:28.530 --> 00:42:30.560
essentially as soon as you breed them out,

00:42:30.560 --> 00:42:33.860
as they dry out, the
particle size changes.

00:42:33.860 --> 00:42:37.310
So there's been a, and
I think this last year,

00:42:37.310 --> 00:42:39.500
there was a fantastic national

00:42:39.500 --> 00:42:42.630
academy of engineering workshop

00:42:42.630 --> 00:42:45.140
that brought aerosol
scientists and engineers

00:42:45.140 --> 00:42:47.330
together with virologists,

00:42:47.330 --> 00:42:49.780
and physicians and
infection control people.

00:42:49.780 --> 00:42:52.170
So they're are starting to
break down those barriers

00:42:52.170 --> 00:42:53.710
and understand each other.

00:42:53.710 --> 00:42:57.120
So I think the science of how viruses

00:42:57.120 --> 00:42:59.800
or pathogens are spread through the air

00:42:59.800 --> 00:43:03.951
is a absolutely fascinating
field where lots has to happen.

00:43:03.951 --> 00:43:07.680
Cause we, I mean, I'm hearing about

00:43:08.570 --> 00:43:12.310
built environments and you
know, how do you ventilate?

00:43:12.310 --> 00:43:15.100
Because normally we ventilate
in indoor environments

00:43:15.100 --> 00:43:17.313
for comfort, not for health.

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:21.450
And so there is a lot that
has to happen in that space,

00:43:21.450 --> 00:43:23.870
and I think the public health community

00:43:23.870 --> 00:43:25.800
was the slowest to the party.

00:43:25.800 --> 00:43:27.200
There was an unwillingness

00:43:27.200 --> 00:43:30.370
to use the word airborne transmission,

00:43:30.370 --> 00:43:33.570
even though the data was
staring us in our faces.

00:43:33.570 --> 00:43:36.280
And so, I think it had to do

00:43:36.280 --> 00:43:38.520
with the number of complex issues

00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:42.800
such as if you label something
airborne transmissible,

00:43:42.800 --> 00:43:46.500
then it changes the type of
personal protective equipment

00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:48.820
that you need to then provide to people,

00:43:48.820 --> 00:43:51.110
and there was deemed
to be a shortage of it.

00:43:51.110 --> 00:43:54.483
So how could you recommend
that if you couldn't supply it?

00:43:55.360 --> 00:43:58.350
But I think it is time to sort
of step back from all of that

00:43:58.350 --> 00:43:59.669
and look at the data,

00:43:59.669 --> 00:44:02.770
talk to our colleagues in other fields,

00:44:02.770 --> 00:44:06.160
and really break down some of
these barriers and understand

00:44:06.160 --> 00:44:09.453
what is happening and how we
can mitigate some of this.

00:44:10.860 --> 00:44:14.040
- Yeah. Well, you mentioned
talking to colleagues

00:44:14.040 --> 00:44:15.580
in other fields,

00:44:15.580 --> 00:44:17.550
and it seems to me that

00:44:17.550 --> 00:44:20.910
science has gotten
incredibly collaborative

00:44:20.910 --> 00:44:25.210
across fields and across
the world, you know,

00:44:25.210 --> 00:44:28.140
huge numbers of scientists coming together

00:44:28.140 --> 00:44:30.303
to work on these problems.

00:44:31.770 --> 00:44:34.890
You know, what's you guys' perspective?

00:44:34.890 --> 00:44:37.680
Is it, have you seen that,

00:44:37.680 --> 00:44:40.540
or are you seeing soloing?

00:44:40.540 --> 00:44:43.040
What's going on in terms of

00:44:43.040 --> 00:44:46.143
how this pandemic may
be changing research?

00:44:47.575 --> 00:44:50.600
- Yeah, I think that's one
of the biggest changes that

00:44:50.600 --> 00:44:53.000
I've experienced personally is that,

00:44:53.000 --> 00:44:54.940
you know, prior to the pandemic,

00:44:54.940 --> 00:44:59.830
we were pretty much doing
things in silos, as scientists,

00:44:59.830 --> 00:45:02.140
but with the pandemic,

00:45:02.140 --> 00:45:06.000
we've reached out to everyone
who has the expertise

00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:09.500
and who's asking the questions
that you're interested in,

00:45:09.500 --> 00:45:12.630
not just throughout the school
or throughout the states,

00:45:12.630 --> 00:45:14.460
but throughout the world.

00:45:14.460 --> 00:45:18.560
So now we have multiple
collaborations all across the globe,

00:45:18.560 --> 00:45:21.690
really looking at anything from, you know,

00:45:21.690 --> 00:45:25.660
immune pathogenesis, to
vaccine effectiveness,

00:45:25.660 --> 00:45:29.090
to, you know, third dose, you know,

00:45:29.090 --> 00:45:30.372
what is mix and match,

00:45:30.372 --> 00:45:35.372
many, many questions, we can
only address globally together.

00:45:35.670 --> 00:45:38.880
And that's been a very
welcoming change for me.

00:45:38.880 --> 00:45:42.290
Like this team science
is really the future.

00:45:42.290 --> 00:45:45.010
And I think the scientific community,

00:45:45.010 --> 00:45:48.270
like academic community
has to catch up with this

00:45:48.270 --> 00:45:51.750
in terms of evaluating scientists

00:45:51.750 --> 00:45:55.080
The old traditional metrics
about who's the last author,

00:45:55.080 --> 00:45:56.720
who's the first author,

00:45:56.720 --> 00:45:59.760
this kind of measurements is not conducive

00:45:59.760 --> 00:46:03.520
to kind of promoting
this team science effort.

00:46:03.520 --> 00:46:06.690
And so that's something
that I think the community,

00:46:06.690 --> 00:46:09.490
the scientific academic sort of

00:46:09.490 --> 00:46:11.563
environment has to change to accommodate.

00:46:12.970 --> 00:46:15.543
- Do you think it will
last beyond the pandemic?

00:46:16.800 --> 00:46:19.730
- I hope so. I mean, once
you do the team science,

00:46:19.730 --> 00:46:21.357
you can't go backwards.

00:46:21.357 --> 00:46:22.750
(Kanta and Akiko laughing)

00:46:22.750 --> 00:46:23.860
- I think if,

00:46:23.860 --> 00:46:25.220
a lot of,

00:46:25.220 --> 00:46:27.700
I agree with the Akiko,
this has been fantastic

00:46:27.700 --> 00:46:29.560
and some countries have done it

00:46:29.560 --> 00:46:31.500
kind of operationally at a national level.

00:46:31.500 --> 00:46:34.210
Like the UK has done some phenominal

00:46:34.210 --> 00:46:36.810
clinical trials and clinical evaluation

00:46:36.810 --> 00:46:38.823
by forming consortia.

00:46:39.800 --> 00:46:42.380
And so it's not just organically.

00:46:42.380 --> 00:46:43.910
I mean, it's happened
because the governments

00:46:43.910 --> 00:46:44.900
throw money at the problem.

00:46:44.900 --> 00:46:47.410
So, I think the money that's
been thrown at the problem

00:46:47.410 --> 00:46:48.760
has helped a lot.

00:46:48.760 --> 00:46:51.020
And I think my only worry

00:46:51.020 --> 00:46:54.600
about the longevity of
this kind of cohesiveness

00:46:54.600 --> 00:46:56.380
is if money dries up,

00:46:56.380 --> 00:46:57.540
and we again end up

00:46:57.540 --> 00:46:59.860
being in a competitive environment again.

00:46:59.860 --> 00:47:02.460
So I think that, you
know, funding agencies,

00:47:02.460 --> 00:47:05.060
if they can, actually, if they take away

00:47:06.197 --> 00:47:09.600
the funding as the reason to compete,

00:47:09.600 --> 00:47:13.203
then there'll be able to
have much more collaboration.

00:47:14.070 --> 00:47:16.650
- I have seen the rise
and fall and rise and fall

00:47:16.650 --> 00:47:19.693
from SARS and MERS.

00:47:21.030 --> 00:47:23.240
I think this is different
because it's a pandemic.

00:47:23.240 --> 00:47:24.640
I think it's incorporating something.

00:47:24.640 --> 00:47:29.140
My question is, are the Akikos
going to stay in the field?

00:47:29.140 --> 00:47:31.860
Are they going to stay
interested in virology

00:47:31.860 --> 00:47:35.000
as the pressures on it and
become more competitive?

00:47:35.000 --> 00:47:37.340
That wasn't targeted for you of course,

00:47:37.340 --> 00:47:40.030
but it's because it's so important
to have you in the field,

00:47:40.030 --> 00:47:45.000
and that we were a field that
had one or two immunologists

00:47:45.000 --> 00:47:48.050
and two or three replication people,

00:47:48.050 --> 00:47:52.520
and, you know, three or four
structural biologists maybe

00:47:52.520 --> 00:47:53.503
if we were lucky.

00:47:54.570 --> 00:47:57.270
And the question is, I think
it's about infrastructure.

00:47:57.270 --> 00:47:59.460
It's like bridges. It's
like, do you build them?

00:47:59.460 --> 00:48:00.960
And then do you maintain them?

00:48:02.250 --> 00:48:04.460
Because I think the answer is correct.

00:48:04.460 --> 00:48:07.870
I've had more opportunities
in the last year to talk to

00:48:09.744 --> 00:48:13.130
endonuclease and exonuclease biochemists.

00:48:13.130 --> 00:48:15.160
People who are developing small molecules

00:48:15.160 --> 00:48:16.990
fragment based screening,

00:48:16.990 --> 00:48:19.960
novel biochemical
strategies that I ever have.

00:48:19.960 --> 00:48:21.890
I've had people reach and want my,

00:48:21.890 --> 00:48:24.100
all of my MHV reporter viruses,

00:48:24.100 --> 00:48:25.970
because they now want to
look at the cell biology

00:48:25.970 --> 00:48:28.500
of coronaviruses in a BSL-2 model.

00:48:28.500 --> 00:48:30.740
And they want to try to
understand issues of entry

00:48:30.740 --> 00:48:31.600
and so that, you know,

00:48:31.600 --> 00:48:32.433
things that I thought

00:48:32.433 --> 00:48:33.730
I was going to be putting in the autoclave

00:48:33.730 --> 00:48:34.690
at the end of my career,

00:48:34.690 --> 00:48:37.726
I'm passing out around the
world and I'm thrilled,

00:48:37.726 --> 00:48:39.050
but I'll tell you the most important thing

00:48:39.050 --> 00:48:43.100
I think is not for people like
me to establish team science,

00:48:43.100 --> 00:48:45.320
it's to have the David Martinez,

00:48:45.320 --> 00:48:47.610
and the other people
who come into the field

00:48:47.610 --> 00:48:49.720
and who find the capacity to stay.

00:48:49.720 --> 00:48:52.240
So what are the issues
associated with that?

00:48:52.240 --> 00:48:54.500
Well, there's a couple
major ones that I see.

00:48:54.500 --> 00:48:56.720
One is the stability of funding,

00:48:56.720 --> 00:48:58.650
and that they can count on that.

00:48:58.650 --> 00:49:02.181
Two, is that they find that,

00:49:02.181 --> 00:49:04.470
that they can carry that work forward

00:49:04.470 --> 00:49:06.990
in a way that's meaningful,

00:49:06.990 --> 00:49:08.580
if they weren't doing the hard work.

00:49:08.580 --> 00:49:10.160
So, you know, people ask me,

00:49:10.160 --> 00:49:12.561
how did you stick with
coronavirus for 36 years?

00:49:12.561 --> 00:49:13.394
And my wife wanted to say,

00:49:13.394 --> 00:49:14.720
"Well, he's an idiot."

00:49:14.720 --> 00:49:15.861
That might've been the answer

00:49:15.861 --> 00:49:20.861
but I think the idea is that
the ideas compel you so much,

00:49:21.050 --> 00:49:22.610
and you're willing to
fight for those ideas

00:49:22.610 --> 00:49:24.070
and go to the fundamental science.

00:49:24.070 --> 00:49:25.820
Because if everybody in the world

00:49:25.820 --> 00:49:28.470
is looking at the RBD
on the spike protein,

00:49:28.470 --> 00:49:30.070
that isn't going to last, right?

00:49:30.070 --> 00:49:32.010
It's not going to last
because people have to look at

00:49:32.010 --> 00:49:33.250
more fundamental questions,

00:49:33.250 --> 00:49:35.730
that kind that David is approaching.

00:49:35.730 --> 00:49:37.989
And the final one is,
I think we need, yeah.

00:49:37.989 --> 00:49:39.830
We just need young investigators

00:49:39.830 --> 00:49:41.840
and we need to support
them through the process

00:49:41.840 --> 00:49:44.040
and allow them the time to develop,

00:49:44.040 --> 00:49:46.100
and stay in these fields
and make them understand

00:49:46.100 --> 00:49:48.230
that it's an area they can thrive in.

00:49:48.230 --> 00:49:49.063
That's what matters

00:49:49.063 --> 00:49:50.990
Honestly, to me, that's
the issue of continuity

00:49:50.990 --> 00:49:52.090
that matters the most.

00:49:54.080 --> 00:49:55.890
- Makes me almost feel a little bit guilty

00:49:55.890 --> 00:49:57.950
because I was a virologist,

00:49:57.950 --> 00:50:01.670
and then I left to become
a science journalist.

00:50:01.670 --> 00:50:04.210
- Yeah, well. You may have
left for very positive reasons,

00:50:04.210 --> 00:50:05.540
but a lot of people leave

00:50:05.540 --> 00:50:08.250
because they don't feel
it's a sustainable career

00:50:08.250 --> 00:50:10.300
over the long haul, and that's a problem.

00:50:11.660 --> 00:50:12.493
- Yeah.

00:50:12.493 --> 00:50:15.663
Erin became a science journalist

00:50:15.663 --> 00:50:18.910
doing your internship with us

00:50:18.910 --> 00:50:22.260
just at the point where
the pandemic was hitting.

00:50:22.260 --> 00:50:27.053
So you're sort of the
perfect person to have,

00:50:29.060 --> 00:50:30.010
at a very bad time,

00:50:30.010 --> 00:50:32.363
but perfect for us,

00:50:33.500 --> 00:50:36.480
which, you know, she
was really instrumental

00:50:36.480 --> 00:50:40.863
in helping us properly communicate
the virology about this.

00:50:42.180 --> 00:50:44.280
- We have done a terrible job,

00:50:44.280 --> 00:50:46.220
we've done a terrible job as scientists

00:50:46.220 --> 00:50:49.100
of communicating the terminology

00:50:49.100 --> 00:50:52.430
and doing it in a way
that is recognizable,

00:50:52.430 --> 00:50:55.650
is understandable to people
and that engages them

00:50:55.650 --> 00:50:57.310
and lets them understand it's complex.

00:50:57.310 --> 00:50:59.500
And so it's not a contradiction to say,

00:50:59.500 --> 00:51:01.290
one day, we're developing a vaccine

00:51:01.290 --> 00:51:02.560
to try to prevent infection.

00:51:02.560 --> 00:51:04.070
And then we say, well, it
didn't prevent infection,

00:51:04.070 --> 00:51:07.090
but boy is it sure accomplishing
the goal of preventing

00:51:07.090 --> 00:51:09.698
severe disease and
hospitalization and death.

00:51:09.698 --> 00:51:13.730
And that science evolves,
and that it's not static.

00:51:13.730 --> 00:51:15.660
- If I could just add a point that

00:51:15.660 --> 00:51:16.990
perhaps hasn't been mentioned.

00:51:16.990 --> 00:51:19.300
So to echo Mark, I would say

00:51:19.300 --> 00:51:22.560
of course, understanding
the fundamental biology

00:51:22.560 --> 00:51:27.560
of not just coronaviruses,
but other viruses.

00:51:27.940 --> 00:51:31.495
I think it's absolutely critical,
but if I could just add,

00:51:31.495 --> 00:51:35.250
maybe an overarching theme
that we need to rethink

00:51:35.250 --> 00:51:39.220
and maybe reinvent and redesign is,

00:51:39.220 --> 00:51:43.240
how do we change our pandemic
preparedness landscape?

00:51:43.240 --> 00:51:44.630
So instead of sitting,

00:51:44.630 --> 00:51:46.200
instead of being a sitting duck,

00:51:46.200 --> 00:51:49.776
just waiting for a virus to emerge into a

00:51:49.776 --> 00:51:52.280
completely naive human population is,

00:51:52.280 --> 00:51:55.820
I think we need to start prioritizing

00:51:55.820 --> 00:52:00.420
in investing more in pandemic preparedness

00:52:00.420 --> 00:52:02.210
on a more global level,

00:52:02.210 --> 00:52:05.040
not just pandemic preparedness
in terms of vaccine

00:52:05.040 --> 00:52:07.900
and monoclonal antibody
or therapeutic design,

00:52:07.900 --> 00:52:10.220
but broad spectrum antivirals,

00:52:10.220 --> 00:52:12.590
and even just public health infrastructure

00:52:12.590 --> 00:52:17.590
to be able to rapidly
respond to a viral pandemic,

00:52:18.060 --> 00:52:19.513
or a pathogen pandemic.

00:52:20.510 --> 00:52:22.360
- I mean, to get a little
bit back to the idea that,

00:52:22.360 --> 00:52:24.493
I mean, science communication is hard.

00:52:25.730 --> 00:52:28.170
All of you have talked in some capacity

00:52:28.170 --> 00:52:32.100
to journalists like us
throughout the pandemic.

00:52:32.100 --> 00:52:34.360
What are some of the biggest challenges

00:52:34.360 --> 00:52:37.420
that you guys have faced
in either communicating

00:52:37.420 --> 00:52:41.769
your research or just talking
about the science in general?

00:52:41.769 --> 00:52:44.850
- Well, so I use multiple
different platforms

00:52:44.850 --> 00:52:46.053
to communicate.

00:52:47.070 --> 00:52:50.180
Twitter is the fastest. The moment I type

00:52:50.180 --> 00:52:53.020
people can see what I'm talking about.

00:52:53.020 --> 00:52:56.860
I've also sort of engaged
with more traditional media,

00:52:56.860 --> 00:53:00.150
newspapers, television,
radio shows, and so on.

00:53:00.150 --> 00:53:05.150
And I actually decided in
early pandemic to dedicate

00:53:05.440 --> 00:53:09.200
a big portion of my life to
doing science communication,

00:53:09.200 --> 00:53:13.930
because I think it's important
for scientists to speak,

00:53:13.930 --> 00:53:16.518
you know, from experts opinion,

00:53:16.518 --> 00:53:18.130
you know, what what's going on,

00:53:18.130 --> 00:53:21.760
explaining to the lay public,
what is an immune system?

00:53:21.760 --> 00:53:24.140
You know, what does antibody mean?

00:53:24.140 --> 00:53:25.820
How does memory work?

00:53:25.820 --> 00:53:29.390
All these basic fundamental insights that

00:53:29.390 --> 00:53:31.650
many people didn't have prior to pandemic.

00:53:31.650 --> 00:53:33.610
Now people are talking about

00:53:33.610 --> 00:53:35.120
neutralizing antibody titers,

00:53:35.120 --> 00:53:36.780
and RBDs, and, you know,

00:53:36.780 --> 00:53:40.650
they have the lingo to be able
to understand and talk about

00:53:41.840 --> 00:53:43.770
immune system as well as viruses.

00:53:43.770 --> 00:53:47.700
But I think that was important to me

00:53:47.700 --> 00:53:50.500
that we as scientists
communicate with the public.

00:53:50.500 --> 00:53:53.063
And I think all of us try to do that,

00:53:54.150 --> 00:53:55.160
more than ever.

00:53:55.160 --> 00:53:58.790
Like I've never been in
contact with so many reporters

00:53:58.790 --> 00:54:00.420
prior to pandemic.

00:54:00.420 --> 00:54:02.820
Occasionally I would talk
to them about this and that,

00:54:02.820 --> 00:54:06.260
but this is just completely
changed the landscape.

00:54:06.260 --> 00:54:09.990
And I just hope that more
people will engage in this

00:54:09.990 --> 00:54:14.320
because I think the society
needs scientists' voice,

00:54:14.320 --> 00:54:16.340
needs our expertise and, you know,

00:54:16.340 --> 00:54:20.250
needs people to explain to them, you know,

00:54:20.250 --> 00:54:21.920
about what's the future of pandemic?

00:54:21.920 --> 00:54:23.273
How do we contain it?

00:54:24.400 --> 00:54:25.233
All these issues.

00:54:25.233 --> 00:54:27.120
So I think science communication

00:54:27.120 --> 00:54:29.173
is a big part of being a scientist.

00:54:31.320 --> 00:54:33.160
- And I do think that has going to,

00:54:33.160 --> 00:54:35.309
that comes back to what
David was talking about,

00:54:35.309 --> 00:54:37.210
about preparing for the
next pandemic, right?

00:54:37.210 --> 00:54:39.640
Because at the beginning of all this,

00:54:39.640 --> 00:54:42.800
the communication was not amazing.

00:54:42.800 --> 00:54:45.380
And so, you know, when
the next pandemic happens,

00:54:45.380 --> 00:54:49.950
because it will, hopefully
we'll be more prepared.

00:54:49.950 --> 00:54:52.940
- So, I think communication
is absolutely critical

00:54:52.940 --> 00:54:57.160
and scientists are often not
taught how to communicate.

00:54:57.160 --> 00:54:59.793
So, you know, I was at
CDC and NIH and had,

00:55:01.200 --> 00:55:06.070
you know, communications
workshops to attend.

00:55:06.070 --> 00:55:08.680
Pardon me, just so you don't
embarrass the government,

00:55:08.680 --> 00:55:11.450
but University of Melbourne has one too.

00:55:11.450 --> 00:55:13.793
but I think to me probably,

00:55:14.720 --> 00:55:15.553
what we could have,

00:55:15.553 --> 00:55:20.160
I think the public is
actually has been educated

00:55:20.160 --> 00:55:23.150
very much so in the last year and a half,

00:55:23.150 --> 00:55:25.210
mRNA rolls off people's tongues,

00:55:25.210 --> 00:55:27.820
they all know about
boosting and, you know.

00:55:27.820 --> 00:55:30.290
So it is amazing how much

00:55:30.290 --> 00:55:33.930
the general public knows of science now,

00:55:33.930 --> 00:55:37.192
but I think what we probably

00:55:37.192 --> 00:55:39.930
should have communicated better,

00:55:39.930 --> 00:55:43.010
and this is, you know,
journalists as well as scientists,

00:55:43.010 --> 00:55:46.760
is that we were dealing with
something completely new,

00:55:46.760 --> 00:55:50.010
and that the information
was going to change.

00:55:50.010 --> 00:55:54.460
And that it is our job to
be open to new information

00:55:54.460 --> 00:55:58.690
and to change our thinking
as information evolves.

00:55:58.690 --> 00:56:01.010
That simply changing what we're saying

00:56:01.010 --> 00:56:04.610
doesn't mean that we are untrustworthy.

00:56:04.610 --> 00:56:06.860
That we have to find a way to communicate

00:56:06.860 --> 00:56:09.700
that trust should still be maintained

00:56:09.700 --> 00:56:12.350
and that we're responding
to new information.

00:56:12.350 --> 00:56:14.720
So, unfortunately very often

00:56:15.720 --> 00:56:18.580
we are asked to give a definitive answer,

00:56:18.580 --> 00:56:21.350
and it's almost phrased-termed
as if, you know,

00:56:21.350 --> 00:56:23.200
if you've changed, what you're saying,

00:56:23.200 --> 00:56:26.400
that the previous version was false,

00:56:26.400 --> 00:56:29.113
and it's rather that we're
fine tuning it as we go.

00:56:31.241 --> 00:56:34.560
- I've given answers
sometimes that are provocative

00:56:34.560 --> 00:56:36.210
and that the people don't like.

00:56:36.210 --> 00:56:37.043
They say,

00:56:37.043 --> 00:56:37.876
"How long is this going to last?"

00:56:37.876 --> 00:56:38.709
and going,

00:56:38.709 --> 00:56:40.072
"I don't think you want to ask me that."

00:56:40.072 --> 00:56:41.697
Or they'll say,

00:56:41.697 --> 00:56:42.530
"What are you working on now?"

00:56:42.530 --> 00:56:43.363
I say,

00:56:43.363 --> 00:56:44.590
"SARS-CoV-3."

00:56:44.590 --> 00:56:46.905
because we're trying to
prepare for the next virus.

00:56:46.905 --> 00:56:50.670
And it's not really being clear what,

00:56:50.670 --> 00:56:51.523
the point I'm trying to make, you know,

00:56:51.523 --> 00:56:52.610
when I tell people,

00:56:52.610 --> 00:56:54.190
people say how long this is going to last,

00:56:54.190 --> 00:56:56.240
I said, well, the virus will let us know.

00:56:57.485 --> 00:57:00.685
Because I knew, it came
back to some of what Kanta

00:57:00.685 --> 00:57:01.647
was saying earlier.

00:57:01.647 --> 00:57:02.480
We were talking about earlier

00:57:02.480 --> 00:57:04.740
about the virus evolution we don't know.

00:57:04.740 --> 00:57:05.820
We just don't know.

00:57:05.820 --> 00:57:07.980
And I think one thing that I knew,

00:57:07.980 --> 00:57:11.060
but I've learned a new is to be,

00:57:11.060 --> 00:57:13.110
is that we need to have about,

00:57:13.110 --> 00:57:16.160
one part of science and
about nine parts of humility

00:57:16.160 --> 00:57:17.550
around this.

00:57:17.550 --> 00:57:20.840
And that if we're in a
position to tell people this,

00:57:20.840 --> 00:57:21.920
I start my things off.

00:57:21.920 --> 00:57:23.280
I'm going to give you a talk today,

00:57:23.280 --> 00:57:27.260
first I'm going to tell you what I know,

00:57:27.260 --> 00:57:29.750
what we've worked on and
what our results show.

00:57:29.750 --> 00:57:31.600
And I'm going to put those in context.

00:57:31.600 --> 00:57:34.200
And then I'm going to
tell you what I think,

00:57:34.200 --> 00:57:36.340
and then I'm going to tell
you what I have no idea,

00:57:36.340 --> 00:57:38.687
but is my speculation about this.

00:57:38.687 --> 00:57:41.410
And I want you to put
those in context because,

00:57:41.410 --> 00:57:42.290
the first is data,

00:57:42.290 --> 00:57:43.660
the second is my opinion,

00:57:43.660 --> 00:57:47.120
and the third is my worry about it.

00:57:47.120 --> 00:57:50.980
And so then I think that
I've gotten responses back

00:57:50.980 --> 00:57:51.813
from that have been,

00:57:51.813 --> 00:57:52.870
I really appreciated that

00:57:52.870 --> 00:57:54.740
because it allowed me
to put things in context

00:57:54.740 --> 00:57:57.963
of what was really information
and what was opinion,

00:57:58.970 --> 00:58:00.270
what was informed opinion,

00:58:00.270 --> 00:58:02.650
what was speculation
based on what you know,

00:58:03.530 --> 00:58:05.230
and I think that that is,

00:58:05.230 --> 00:58:08.440
it also says that in the face
of a virus that is evolving

00:58:08.440 --> 00:58:10.510
independent of us, couldn't
care less about us,

00:58:10.510 --> 00:58:13.130
that it's going to do what it does.

00:58:13.130 --> 00:58:15.750
And I've been shocked and amazed by

00:58:15.750 --> 00:58:18.783
it's profound, rapid
evolutionary capacity,

00:58:20.100 --> 00:58:21.960
and that we don't know how long it took

00:58:21.960 --> 00:58:25.160
OC43 and HKU1, the human
cold coronaviruses.

00:58:25.160 --> 00:58:26.600
We don't know how long it took for them

00:58:26.600 --> 00:58:28.790
to become the way they became.

00:58:28.790 --> 00:58:30.060
It's not going to happen overnight,

00:58:30.060 --> 00:58:30.893
and people want to say,

00:58:30.893 --> 00:58:32.380
well in a year or so
this is just going to be

00:58:32.380 --> 00:58:33.513
a common cold virus.

00:58:34.973 --> 00:58:36.540
It might be,

00:58:36.540 --> 00:58:39.920
but the statement is nonsense.

00:58:39.920 --> 00:58:42.620
And so I think that just
this kind of humility

00:58:42.620 --> 00:58:45.140
we haven't had, and our
willingness to say, I don't know,

00:58:45.140 --> 00:58:47.450
I don't know. I don't know.

00:58:47.450 --> 00:58:48.780
No one's going to keep interviewing us

00:58:48.780 --> 00:58:50.527
because they'll stop
because we don't know.

00:58:50.527 --> 00:58:51.810
(panelists laughing)

00:58:51.810 --> 00:58:52.708
But it's the truth.

00:58:52.708 --> 00:58:53.541
It's...

00:58:54.926 --> 00:58:56.980
- And I would say, you know,

00:58:56.980 --> 00:58:59.930
adding to Mark's point about, you know,

00:58:59.930 --> 00:59:02.065
just saying what we don't
know and having humility,

00:59:02.065 --> 00:59:05.070
I would add that a strategy

00:59:05.070 --> 00:59:06.970
that has certainly worked for me

00:59:06.970 --> 00:59:10.920
in terms of communicating with the public

00:59:10.920 --> 00:59:15.630
is really trying to communicate
the nuance and science,

00:59:15.630 --> 00:59:18.710
and really not speaking in absolute terms.

00:59:18.710 --> 00:59:22.330
So just, coming with that
humility of saying, you know,

00:59:22.330 --> 00:59:24.887
and just like in real life,

00:59:24.887 --> 00:59:29.887
most things most of the
time are never 100 or 0%.

00:59:30.110 --> 00:59:31.800
There's always a spectrum.

00:59:31.800 --> 00:59:33.150
There are nuances.

00:59:33.150 --> 00:59:36.510
And this is one of those things
that is so new for everyone

00:59:36.510 --> 00:59:39.253
that we just don't know,
and we're learning as we go.

00:59:41.580 --> 00:59:43.410
- Yeah. That is a good point.

00:59:43.410 --> 00:59:46.030
Thank you all for those thoughts.

00:59:46.030 --> 00:59:47.700
We are coming up on the end of our time.

00:59:47.700 --> 00:59:50.080
So I just had one final question,

00:59:50.080 --> 00:59:52.350
'cause we wanted to end on a hopeful note

00:59:52.350 --> 00:59:56.000
after talking about such a grim, you know,

00:59:56.000 --> 00:59:57.423
topic for an hour.

00:59:58.440 --> 01:00:01.030
Maybe very briefly, each
of you could just talk

01:00:01.030 --> 01:00:02.820
really quick about what has given you hope

01:00:02.820 --> 01:00:04.300
over the past two years.

01:00:04.300 --> 01:00:05.560
Like one thing that, you know,

01:00:05.560 --> 01:00:08.133
is your bright light at
the end of the tunnel.

01:00:09.760 --> 01:00:14.380
- Well, what's given me
hope is sort of the future.

01:00:14.380 --> 01:00:17.020
As Mark said, I think

01:00:17.020 --> 01:00:21.237
supporting and encouraging the
new generation of scientists

01:00:21.237 --> 01:00:23.300
is what's giving me hope.

01:00:23.300 --> 01:00:26.790
And I see it in my own eyes,

01:00:26.790 --> 01:00:29.520
my trainees, as well as people around me,

01:00:29.520 --> 01:00:34.400
or young scientists who
are now really engaging in

01:00:34.400 --> 01:00:38.773
the science of long Covid,
science of Covid immunity.

01:00:40.150 --> 01:00:41.410
It really does give me hope,

01:00:41.410 --> 01:00:46.290
and they're so dedicated,
and talented, and driven.

01:00:46.290 --> 01:00:49.180
And that to me is the future.

01:00:49.180 --> 01:00:51.564
And that's how we fight the next pandemic

01:00:51.564 --> 01:00:54.230
is by developing, and growing,

01:00:54.230 --> 01:00:56.940
and nurturing the next
generation of scientists.

01:00:56.940 --> 01:01:00.023
So that's where I see the light.

01:01:02.010 --> 01:01:05.370
- The single most thing
that has given me hope,

01:01:05.370 --> 01:01:09.400
is just watch the community,
just come together.

01:01:09.400 --> 01:01:11.520
The scientific community come together

01:01:11.520 --> 01:01:14.193
with industry partners,

01:01:15.240 --> 01:01:18.670
to which we saw the
development of not just,

01:01:18.670 --> 01:01:21.860
not one, but three safe
and effective vaccines

01:01:21.860 --> 01:01:24.800
in the US alone in less than one year.

01:01:24.800 --> 01:01:29.640
So to me, that's been one of the

01:01:29.640 --> 01:01:32.060
silver linings of this whole thing

01:01:32.060 --> 01:01:37.060
is seeing, you know, if we let
science take the front seat,

01:01:37.520 --> 01:01:40.540
then this is what we can achieve together.

01:01:40.540 --> 01:01:43.370
- I would add that from my standpoint,

01:01:43.370 --> 01:01:47.687
it's the new technologies
and the new team science,

01:01:47.687 --> 01:01:51.570
and the sort of breaking down of barriers

01:01:51.570 --> 01:01:53.763
domestically and internationally.

01:01:54.820 --> 01:01:57.920
And so many people from
many different fields

01:01:57.920 --> 01:02:02.750
have entered this field and
really contributed enormously.

01:02:02.750 --> 01:02:04.510
And that gives me great hope

01:02:04.510 --> 01:02:06.884
that we will see the end of this

01:02:06.884 --> 01:02:11.083
and that it will serve us well
with future health threats.

01:02:13.130 --> 01:02:15.910
- Well, it gives me hope
this particular virus

01:02:15.910 --> 01:02:18.700
is this stunning speed

01:02:18.700 --> 01:02:20.630
of development of vaccines and antivirals.

01:02:20.630 --> 01:02:22.450
People keep saying, why
is it taking so long?

01:02:22.450 --> 01:02:23.908
Well, it didn't.

01:02:23.908 --> 01:02:27.400
Even seven years to
develop antivirals I mean,

01:02:27.400 --> 01:02:30.050
and the fact that we now
understand the targets,

01:02:30.050 --> 01:02:32.790
I think our capacity
to target this is good,

01:02:32.790 --> 01:02:35.280
and it's going to serve us well

01:02:35.280 --> 01:02:36.920
both from vaccines, antivirals,

01:02:36.920 --> 01:02:38.490
understanding of immune response,

01:02:38.490 --> 01:02:40.610
all of this is coming together.

01:02:40.610 --> 01:02:42.820
It's overwhelming for those
of us who try to keep up

01:02:42.820 --> 01:02:43.903
in any area,

01:02:44.810 --> 01:02:46.820
and that's why we need
good science communication

01:02:46.820 --> 01:02:48.160
to help me find things to read

01:02:48.160 --> 01:02:51.283
that I haven't had time to
read the primary literature on.

01:02:52.430 --> 01:02:54.240
But I think this,

01:02:54.240 --> 01:02:56.303
just the fact of the people
that you have on this

01:02:56.303 --> 01:03:01.160
raging across disciplines,
across career stages,

01:03:01.160 --> 01:03:04.410
all focused on a common enemy,

01:03:04.410 --> 01:03:06.520
but a common area of
science and understanding

01:03:06.520 --> 01:03:08.220
that could potentially
lead us into the future

01:03:08.220 --> 01:03:09.053
in new models.

01:03:09.053 --> 01:03:11.853
So, I think that's the most hopeful thing.

01:03:13.330 --> 01:03:15.800
- I think we can all use
some help keeping up with

01:03:15.800 --> 01:03:18.270
things that are happening
in the Covid world.

01:03:18.270 --> 01:03:20.336
It's all moving so fast.

01:03:20.336 --> 01:03:22.940
But again, thank you
all for taking the time

01:03:22.940 --> 01:03:23.773
to talk with us.

01:03:23.773 --> 01:03:25.670
This has been a fascinating conversation.

01:03:25.670 --> 01:03:27.920
I hope you all have
learned as much as we have,

01:03:27.920 --> 01:03:29.993
and how much our viewers will.

01:03:31.300 --> 01:03:33.907
Viewers, if you want to
learn more about Covid-19

01:03:33.907 --> 01:03:37.330
and the coronavirus, you can
always visit our website.

01:03:37.330 --> 01:03:38.943
That's science news.org,

01:03:39.940 --> 01:03:41.950
and connect with us on social media.

01:03:41.950 --> 01:03:43.129
Thanks for joining us.

01:03:43.129 --> 01:03:44.367
- Happy anniversary.

01:03:44.367 --> 01:03:45.230
(air whooshing)

01:03:45.230 --> 01:03:47.813
(upbeat music)

